# Latest Rust features

Workspace floor is `[workspace.package] rust-version` and `edition` in the root `Cargo.toml`. Write for that compiler. Do not write as if MSRV were an older rustc from training data.

When writing or changing Rust, **look up** language, std, and Cargo features stabilized after your knowledge cutoff and up through the workspace MSRV. Training data is stale. Do not rely on memory for what is stable.

Sources: [Rust release notes](https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/releases.html), [Rust blog](https://blog.rust-lang.org/), and current std docs. Check the last several stable releases (1.96, 1.97, 1.98, … — whatever is current), not only the newest one.

Use a newer **stable** feature when it makes this code shorter, clearer, or more efficient (including replacing a crate or a hand-rolled helper with std). Skip it when it does not help, changes semantics, or would require an unrelated mass-refactor. Nightly/`feature` gates are forbidden unless the crate already uses them.
